GS stage queue as a byte ring with one producer (the GS worker, the only caller of push) and one consumer (the
stage thread). In ship combat the worker sends ~240k packets per second; the former mutex + vector queue made both
threads take the lock for every packet and woke the sleeping stage through the kernel for ~60% of them (~150k
context switches per second). Packets are now written into the ring and read in place; the mutex and condition
variables are used only when a side really sleeps, which the stage first avoids by spinning up to
GOW_GS_STAGE_SPIN_US (default 50, 0: sleep at once). The 4 MB bound on queued bytes is kept; packets above 1 MB go
through a heap copy. afterBatch still runs after every batch (the records written when the stage looked).
Measured on top of patch 43 (save route, full screen, 4 alternating pairs): combat 52.8 -> 54.7 FPS, 4 of 4 pairs;
mean 52.8 -> 54.7 FPS.

